    ('Party wall considerations.','Structural work on a semi affects the neighbouring home, whether through shared foundation, shared roof structure, or acoustic transmission. We plan the work to protect the neighbour’s property, coordinate any work that requires their agreement, and put dust and vibration controls in place before demolition starts.'),
    ('Balloon framing and century construction.','Danforth semis were mostly built with balloon-frame construction, meaning wall studs run continuously from the foundation to the roof. This affects fire-blocking, structural modifications, and any electrical or mechanical routing through the walls. We handle it as part of design rather than discovery.'),
    ('Original brickwork.','The brick facades that define the Danforth were built with soft lime mortar and specific brick types that don’t match modern equivalents. Renovations that alter the facade need masons who understand original construction, know how to source or match salvaged brick, and can restore rather than replace.'),
    ('Sound isolation between semis.','Sharing a wall with neighbours means acoustic performance matters. Modern renovation gives us the chance to significantly improve sound isolation through resilient channels, sound-absorbing insulation, and proper detail work at the party wall interface.'),
